21:51:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Corrosive substances—such as strong acids, alkalis, and oxidizing agents—are often stored in glass containers because glass is highly resistant to chemical attack. Here’s why: - **Chemical inertness**: Glass is primarily composed of silica (silicon dioxide, SiO₂), which forms a tightly bonded, three-dimensional network. This structure is largely unreactive with most corrosive chemicals at room temperature, preventing the container from degrading or contaminating the contents. - **Non-porous surface**: Glass has a smooth, non-porous surface that resists absorption and adsorption, so corrosive liquids cannot seep into the material or cause structural weakening over time. - **Transparency**: Clear glass allows safe visual monitoring of the substance’s level, color, and any unwanted changes or reactions without opening the container. - **Wide compatibility**: Glass withstands most acids (e.g., hydrochloric, sulfuric, nitric) and many other aggressive solvents, making it a default laboratory storage material. **Exceptions**: Not all corrosives are safe in glass. Hydrofluoric acid (HF) rapidly etches silica, and concentrated strong alkalis (like hot sodium hydroxide) attack glass over time. Such substances are stored in specialized plastics (e.g., PTFE, polyethylene) instead.
